Magenta is an x86-64 desktop and text interface built on Avalanche Kernel.
Source and applications are covered by ../../avalanche/LICENSE (MIT).

The ISO additionally contains GNU GRUB 2.12-9+deb13u2. Its licensing and
copyright notices are in ../../avalanche/images/GRUB-COPYRIGHT, and the
corresponding upstream source and Debian build rules/patches are included
in ../../avalanche/images/grub-source/. The MIT license does not relicense
GRUB. Kernel capabilities are described in ../../avalanche/images/NOTICE.

The ISO starts the graphical desktop on framebuffer-equipped machines,
with a text desktop available through Escape. The graphical desktop has
Terminal, Files, Text editor, Tasks, Network, Packages and Settings windows.
TrueType fonts, alpha blending, blur, shadows, rounded corners and window
fades are rendered by the native compositor. The DejaVu font license is
in ../../avalanche/assets/FONT-LICENSE.

Widgets use the Nuklear C toolkit and its Catppuccin themes under the MIT
licenses in ../../avalanche/vendor/nuklear/. ORIGIN.md records the upstream
revisions. The desktop layout draws inspiration from COSMIC. Appearance
settings persist themes, accents, wallpapers, transparency, blur and pointer
speed. Terminal runs the shell. The editor asks before discarding changes.
Windows can be moved, resized, minimized, maximized and closed. License
notices for the toolkit, palette, fonts, kernel and bootloader are in the
ISO's /licenses directory.

The ISO boots directly into Magenta; a second boot entry opens the rescue
console. The separate writable data image contains Magenta, the userspace
shell and utilities, Linux ABI examples, and a welcome file. Without a
recognized writable disk, files use RAM. Network settings are per-session.
Menus call kernel file, process, network, device and power services.
The text editor supports ASCII
text and files up to 64 KiB. Binary files have a bounded hexadecimal view.

Magenta, its native applications and its extended kernel are now ELF64
x86-64 binaries. The Linux demos remain ELF32 compatibility applications.
Use a 64-bit virtual machine.
The memory allocator manages RAM below 128 MiB.
